  - THE CARRIED BY IS NEVER THE LINE'S OWN PRESSED VERSE (ASK 355; module ECW, Shared Library line 3500; the E-COL and E-HOLD instructions, library lines 3198 and 3200):
    - What it does: an E-COL push to STANDS, or an E-HOLD push to UNDERDETERMINED, whose CARRIED BY reference is the line's own pressed verse is HELD: recorded and not sent back, with the reason "its CARRIED BY <reference> is the line's own pressed verse; the words in dispute cannot carry a rival reading of themselves; a rival reading is carried by another row read with it (Commitment 16; ASK 355)"; for a FORCED item, ", read from its collision line" follows "pressed verse". The pressed verse is every reference in the parentheses at the head of the collision line (a range covers each verse in it) and every [L1] verse of its GROUND, read outside quotations; for a FORCED item, the same is read from that seat's collision line for the same pair and item. Other verses of the GROUND ([L3] and the like) stay usable as CARRIED BY, so a reading of the verse with the verses read with it (Isaiah 28:10) is not refused. The check runs before the NOT CHECKED path, so a line naming no answering side, or a run with no rounds read, does not escape it.
    - The absence exemption (EX) applies only when the reply line names no CARRIED BY; a line that offers a rival reading goes through the gate.
    - Prompt: the E-COL instructions (Judge Enforcer Build, Alt and R2 Alt) add "The CARRIED BY row is never the line's own pressed verse (a verse named at the head of the line, or the GROUND's [L1] verse; for a FORCED line, those of its collision line): that verse holds the words in dispute, and quoting it again shows no rival reading. Read the answer with every other verse either side quoted on the subject (Isaiah 28:10). A CARRIED BY on the pressed verse is recorded and not sent back (ASK 355)." The E-HOLD instructions add the same rule for an UNDERDETERMINED push.
    - AGREE CARRIED BY (TRIAL, record only): the E-COL instructions ask DeepSeek, when it AGREEs that a link is BROKEN, to end that reply line with "-- CARRIED BY: <reference> "<at least 6 words in a row of that verse, as the record holds it>"", the row whose words the rival reading cannot also hold. No code reads it; an AGREE without it is still an AGREE; the pressed-verse rule does not apply to an AGREE.
    - Shown offline: of the stored E-COL and E-HOLD pushes of runs 37812 and 37891, 8 move from sent to HELD, each quoting the line's own pressed words back as the rival reading; no other push changes. Not shown by a run.
    - Limits: a rival reading of a word inside the pressed verse alone can no longer overturn a break; the enforcer must bring another row. An "[L1]" written after the GROUND, in the line's later text, is read as pressed too. A FORCED item whose seat wrote no collision line for the pair gets no pressed-verse check.
  - A FOOTING PUSH IS NOT SENT AND A FOOTING WITHDRAWAL STAYS UNCURED (ASK 353; Shared Library lines 2647 and 3354, the footing reader ackFoot2024 and the grade-form test ackForm2024; line 3361, ETC; line 3406; line 2688, enf428Cure):
    - Why: in run 37891 DeepSeek pushed sol's FORCED grade to STANDS while its own reason named the defect "missing footing rather than a meaning that cannot agree", and sol's later retraction was cured although its own reason named "a separate assumed-fact defect". A missing fact is not a meaning that can agree, and withdrawing the break does not supply the fact.
    - The footing reader: a footing phrase outside quotations that is not negated (not, never, nor, no, nothing, no longer, isn't, is not or without, within the three words before it). The phrases: missing footing, borrowed footing, no footing (not when defect, fault, gap, problem or issue follows), footing defect, footing fault, footing gap, assumed fact (or assumed-fact), rather than exhibiting, and unexhibited followed within two words by fact. A bare "assumed" or "unexhibited" is not read.
    - The judge's grade form: both parts below apply only when the judge's own flagged line, outside its quotations, holds a footing phrase or an "ASSUMES:" field. A meaning line is untouched whatever the enforcer's reason says.
    - Part A (Judge Enforcer Record, Alt and R2 Alt): an E-COL DISAGREE pushing to STANDS or UNDERDETERMINED whose own reason names a footing defect is recorded CONTRADICTED (pkgETC), right BROKEN, with the reason "its own reason names a footing defect, "<phrase>": a missing fact is not a meaning that can agree, so the push to <grade> is not sent; ASK 353". It is not sent back, and the judge keeps its grade (ruling 428).
    - Part B (Send Back Record, Alt and R2 Alt): a CHANGE TO beginning RETRACTED is a withdrawal even when its text holds BROKEN or FORCED (cure reason "CHANGE TO RETRACTED"). A withdrawal whose own words or reason name a footing defect reads UNCURED: "withdrawn, but its own words name a footing defect ("<phrase>"): a missing fact is not cured by withdrawing the break (ASK 353)". A pkgGRT2 companion item is exempt. Other withdrawals keep their cure text. No cure by a row that gives the missing fact is built yet, so a footing withdrawal stays UNCURED (section 17).
    - Shown offline: over 331 stored E-COL pushes, Part A changes one (run 37891, sol, D4 vs C4); over 314 stored send-backs that parse, Part B changes four: sol's withdrawal in run 37891 goes from CURED to UNCURED, and three RETRACTED lines holding FORCED are now read as retractions (one of them from UNCURED to CURED). Not shown by a run.
    - Limits: a footing defect worded in none of the listed phrases ("the given genealogical fact") is not read; a line beginning RETRACTED that also holds BROKEN is read as a withdrawal.
